From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: gunyah: optionally keep the watchdog running across suspend

The driver stops an active watchdog on system suspend and restarts it on
resume. Gunyah already freezes a VM's watchdog while the VM is suspended
at system level (OSI) and unfreezes it when the first vCPU resumes, so a
watchdog kept running across suspend can catch a VM that resumes but
stalls before Linux's resume path runs.

Add keep_in_suspend (default off) for testing the AYN Odin 3 s2idle
APPS-wake hang. Test only; drop before merging.
---
--- a/drivers/watchdog/gunyah_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/gunyah_wdt.c
@@ -209,11 +209,20 @@ static void gunyah_wdt_remove(struct pla
 	gunyah_wdt_stop(wdd);
 }
 
+/*
+ * s2idle testing: leave an active watchdog running across system suspend.
+ * Gunyah freezes the VM watchdog while the VM is suspended at system level,
+ * so it can then catch a VM that resumes but stalls before Linux's resume.
+ */
+static bool keep_in_suspend;
+module_param(keep_in_suspend, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(keep_in_suspend, "Keep an active watchdog running across system suspend");
+
 static int gunyah_wdt_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct watchdog_device *wdd = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-	if (watchdog_active(wdd))
+	if (watchdog_active(wdd) && !READ_ONCE(keep_in_suspend))
 		gunyah_wdt_stop(wdd);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -223,7 +232,7 @@ static int gunyah_wdt_resume(struct devi
 {
 	struct watchdog_device *wdd = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
-	if (watchdog_active(wdd))
+	if (watchdog_active(wdd) && !READ_ONCE(keep_in_suspend))
 		gunyah_wdt_start(wdd);
 
 	return 0;
